WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.462 --> 00:00:02.170
SPEAKER: Welcome back
to our short series

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.260
on using G Suite APIs.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.440
This video is intended for
anyone interested in preparing

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.510
for their future careers.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.190
And in this episode,
I will walk you

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:14.640
through how to integrate a
web-based ordering system

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.350
with a Google Sheet.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.240
Note that this video's
description contains

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.210
a wonderful Codelab with
this exact use case created

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.380
by my peer, Eric [INAUDIBLE],,
along with other helpful links.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.160
So to start, imagine you work
for a bike-sharing startup that

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.610
has a bike ordering web
app built on Node.js.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.350
If you're familiar
with JavaScript,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.770
Node.js is great since it
runs on JavaScript code

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.370
outside of a browser to develop
things like web servers.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.320
The key functionality
of this app

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.900
is keeping track of
changes in inventory,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.990
unit price, and the
status of each bike order.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.780
The goal now is to have
this information synced

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.690
with a Google Sheet along
with real-time pivot tables

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.090
and charts for
easy visualization.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.580
In order to track the
Sheets created by the app

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.010
and keep the data
synced to the Sheets,

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.650
we generate a table
in our database.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.930
To make this possible,
we use a library

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.320
called Sequelize, which helps
map and synchronize data

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.630
entries to objects
within databases,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:16.220
such as MySQL,
Postgres, et cetera.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.300
In Node, you can
containerize code

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.470
into what is called modules and
then import them using Require

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.790
as well as module.export
for other files to access.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.370
Next, we need to fetch all
of the spreadsheets we've

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.700
stored when the index page
loads so we can display them

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.570
in a list.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.590
And with a bit of HTML, we
will create two buttons,

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.770
one called Create to
generate spreadsheets

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.140
and another button called
Sync to sync that data.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.630
We then move on to downloading
the Google API's Node.js client

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.930
library and the OAuth 2 library.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:57.340
With these, you can create an
access token to get credentials

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.460
and initializes the
Sheets API client.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.730
Next, we'll use the
spreadsheets.create method

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.520
for creating the spreadsheet.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.510
The spreadsheets.batchUpdate
method

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.210
is commonly used for nearly
every type of manipulation

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.760
to a spreadsheet because it
takes an array of request

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.560
objects as an input,
which contains

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.080
specific operations to perform.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.930
In this example, we mapped
the columns headers to fields

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.430
in the databases order object.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.400
Finally, we created
a function, which

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.890
converts the order
objects into cells

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.560
and adds data validation for
the different data types.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.130
For example, the
column status displays

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.980
a dropdown with values,
such as pending,

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.770
shipped, and delivered.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:38.810
And there you go.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.780
We now have our application
exporting to Google Sheets.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.130
Now, I'm waiting for someone to
ask, couldn't we have achieved

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.220
a similar result
exporting the data as CSVs

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.070
and converting them into
Google Sheets manually?

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:54.310
Well, I'm glad you asked, since
the additional benefit in using

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.980
this API-based
approach is that on top

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:00.100
of managing sign-ins
and automatic syncing,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.800
it also has the ability
to add complex features

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.080
to spreadsheets,
such as pivot tables

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.360
and charts automatically.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.730
Let me show you this
in this final piece.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.270
Note that a best practice is
to have two different Sheets

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:15.550
created within a
spreadsheet, one

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:19.120
for the raw data and the
second to display the charts.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.980
This allows for the
code used for syncing

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:25.100
to focus on the data exclusively
and not on the visualizations.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.420
So let's return to our earlier
create spreadsheet method

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.540
and add the following properties
for creating a pivot table.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:34.600
We will also need to capture
the ID of that second Sheet

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.270
to build the following
three requests, which

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:39.910
build the pivot table,
format the pivot table,

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.540
and create a chart.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.340
Let's take a quick look at each.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.870
So this is what the build
pivot table request looks like,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:49.060
which describes the
columns and values that

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.500
should be summarized.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.810
Then, there's the
build form pivot table

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:57.760
request, which ensures that
data is formatted in a currency.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.390
And the last one is the
build/add chart request,

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:05.470
which creates a bar chart
called Revenue per Product.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.140
And there we have it, a
lovely synchronous process

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:10.860
between a database
and visualizations

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.400
in Google Spreadsheets.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.070
My name's [INAUDIBLE] and this
has been a brief introduction

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:16.819
to G Suite's developer tools.

00:04:16.819 --> 00:04:17.319
Cheers.

00:04:17.319 --> 00:04:20.669
[MUSIC PLAYING]

